WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.000
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome and Newman
to the program today, author and columnist,

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.580
and we are going to be talking about vaccines,
operation, warp speed.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.880
There are really so many things to discuss.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.559
And so maybe, maybe to start Operation Warp
Speed.

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:21.460
I interviewed a Republican strategist recently
and she very adamantly pointed to Operation

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.040
Warp Speed and Donald Trump as the reason
why so many vaccines are now available.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.260
I asked exactly what Trump did.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.680
She didn't really have anything to tell me.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.130
So let's start there.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:37.310
I mean, what exactly did Operation Warp Speed
achieve that wouldn't otherwise have been

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:38.310
achieved?

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:39.310
What was Trump's role in that?

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:46.780
Speaker 6: Yeah, Trump set this up as instead
of opening up the market to the world and

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.280
trying to make these formulas accessible to
everyone.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:55.460
He kind of made this pool of money that incentivized
six individual companies.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:01.370
We're talking ten billion dollars, probably
up to 18 billion at this point.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:06.180
And these six companies received a huge amount
of money as well as exclusivity.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:14.230
So they were paid, say, one hundred and ninety
one point nine, five billion dollars for research.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:15.230
Yes.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.360
But then also an additional amount for vaccines.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:24.470
And those vaccines, that payment was guaranteed,
whether the vaccines worked or not.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:29.870
So what it did was pull a whole bunch of minds
in one place and dump tons of money into them

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.800
with this concept that a free market, which,
of course, is not free.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.800
Right.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.690
That's like the perfect example of a form
of socialism.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:37.690
Yeah.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:43.370
What would accelerate the development of these
vaccines?

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:49.130
Meanwhile, the Mornay research was already
being worked on by the National Institutes

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.270
of Health.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:56.659
So like all pharmaceutical innovation, this
was grandly subsidized not just monetarily,

00:01:56.659 --> 00:02:00.490
but also research wise by by federal entities.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:07.200
Speaker 1: Now, I've I've read I read things
that are essentially it seems as though both

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.390
can't possibly be true.

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:14.670
On the one hand, I'm reading about how the
pharmaceutical companies are not releasing

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:19.430
the formula for manufacturing vaccine so that
other countries could just start producing

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.500
them right away rather than waiting on the
supply flow from the companies that are producing

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.500
them.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.530
Now, at the same time, I read that, for example,
in twenty twenty, I don't remember if it was

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:34.090
Pfizer, Moderna or both said that they were
open sourcing the recipe if someone wants

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:35.420
to produce it.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.530
But it does seem as though there is significant
infrastructure and fixed costs necessary to

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:45.520
do that, where maybe it's not actually going
to get vaccines to people more quickly.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.849
If a new country wants to start manufacturing,
what is the truth about that?

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:58.660
Speaker 6: Yeah, it's it's really hard to
pass because the IP patent special manufacturing

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:04.891
categories also there's a category, undisclosed
information like since the Reagan era, pharmaceutical

00:03:04.891 --> 00:03:11.790
companies have employed a whole raft of special
categories that are covered and set up to

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.130
protect their profit, basically.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:19.910
And so even though Moderna last fall, I believe
it was in October, as you mentioned, said,

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:26.340
sure enough, our patent is we won't enforce
our patent is what they said.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.340
Yes, it was a great PR play for them, but
it basically didn't do anything because there

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:37.050
are these other layers of intellectual property
enforcement that can be employed whenever

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.450
anyone gets involved.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:44.090
And, you know, I talked to Dean Baker for
the article that I wrote for The Baffler.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:50.390
And Baker, who is an expert on intellectual
property, said, you know, they there are many

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:57.780
ways that the government could make this information
available.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:02.510
So intellectual property is being used to
cover not just the formula for the recipes,

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:11.030
but also these techniques and manufacturing
processes that really could just be disclosed

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:12.580
as easily as any recipe.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:19.099
I mean, there's nothing that prevents manufacturers
of pharmaceuticals from sharing that information

00:04:19.099 --> 00:04:22.139
as well, except that they're getting ridiculously
rich.

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:27.720
Speaker 1: Now, as a practical question, I'll
give you one situation that's happening in

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.969
Argentina where I was born and have a lot
of family.

00:04:30.969 --> 00:04:38.350
Argentina is primarily using the Sputnik vaccine,
which many love because it's open source.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:42.809
And now Argentina has announced that they
are going to start manufacturing it.

00:04:42.809 --> 00:04:47.530
And some of my uncles are very, very happy
who are loyalists to the current administration.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:51.870
But when I actually look into the details,
the ramp, if it's even true.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:55.020
Right, let's assume it's true that Argentina
will start manufacturing this.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.770
If it's even true, the ramp up process will
take so long that.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:04.699
By the time Argentina could be making any
of these Sputnik vaccines, Argentina won't

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:09.800
need them and it will really be used to take
something that's open source and sell it to

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.770
even poorer countries in South America to
make money in Argentina.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:21.449
So is like is is that good or is the scale
up process really even possible to do quickly

00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:26.189
enough that it wouldn't just make sense to
get an order in for vaccines rather than look

00:05:26.189 --> 00:05:27.469
at making them oneself?

00:05:27.469 --> 00:05:29.189
I'm just thinking out loud here.

00:05:29.189 --> 00:05:33.749
Speaker 6: Well, like anything else, it's
distribution and information, right?

00:05:33.749 --> 00:05:39.039
That's what hinders the the sharing of any
kind of material.

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:47.110
And so distribution access to the APIs or
the active pharmaceutical ingredients, ingredients,

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:53.550
the technology and the materials could be
better negotiated.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:54.550
Right.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:06:01.469
There's no central power that is making sure
that the countries who need this technology

00:06:01.469 --> 00:06:03.960
and these formulas are going to get them.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:04.960
And that's the real problem.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:09.249
I mean, there are a number of organizations
that at the very beginning said, all right,

00:06:09.249 --> 00:06:10.879
this is going to be big.

00:06:10.879 --> 00:06:17.210
And as a globe, we need to work together to
to make sure that everyone has the same access,

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:23.069
that medical ethics is employed in the distribution
of these very necessary medicines.

00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:26.659
And obviously, Bill Gates came in.

00:06:26.659 --> 00:06:31.669
Who's playing this unnecessarily large role
in public health, health around the globe

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:33.539
with an extraordinary amount of goodwill.

00:06:33.539 --> 00:06:39.689
And he basically co-opted the system to make
it so that everything was run through large

00:06:39.689 --> 00:06:41.900
pharmaceutical companies.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.060
And so here we are with this crazy bottleneck.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:49.529
I also want to emphasize that in other countries
around the world, there are other issues I.

00:06:49.529 --> 00:06:57.000
I don't want to take India's Modi off the
table as a stumbling block.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.279
People are making bad decisions all around
the world.

00:06:59.279 --> 00:07:05.020
We also know that on other continents, including
our own, there are corrupt governments and

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:07.030
corrupt politicians.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:12.999
And so I think on the whole, as a as a globe,
we were completely.

00:07:12.999 --> 00:07:18.860
Unprepared for what was coming our way and
we never had a strong voice, the United States

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:23.610
never demonstrated any kind of leadership,
and certainly during the Trump administration,

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:32.039
we were incredibly isolationist in how our
politics were discussed and and and mediated.

00:07:32.039 --> 00:07:36.779
So I think that America first isolationism
also put us very much behind.

00:07:36.779 --> 00:07:41.330
So it's not just the fact that these vaccines
are hard to get a hold of because of patents

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:43.800
and these layers of IP protection.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:49.509
But it's that also the you know, just the
the understanding of we need to work together

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:51.490
to make sure this happens just isn't there.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:58.800
Speaker 1: When it comes to the dramatic increase
in actual vaccinations that has taken place

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:05.819
over the last few few months, was this naturally
going to happen no matter who was president,

00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:11.189
or does the Biden administration get credit
for what they've what they've done?

00:08:11.189 --> 00:08:15.759
Speaker 6: I think that's a good question,
I think certainly Biden coming in removed

00:08:15.759 --> 00:08:17.759
a lot of the animosity.

00:08:17.759 --> 00:08:19.219
He's been clear with messaging.

00:08:19.219 --> 00:08:23.199
I think there was so much misinformation,
whether that's by design in our current media

00:08:23.199 --> 00:08:28.279
landscape or just an administration that really
didn't care about the rest of the world dying,

00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:33.090
didn't care about the disparity in the United
States, where blacks and people of color were

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:36.270
unable to access vaccine doses.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:41.120
So I think definitely it's a change and tone
and tenor a lot.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:46.900
But, yeah, pharmaceuticals are going to pharmaceutical
and they are working nondisclosure contracts

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.270
with countries around the world.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:50.300
That's a huge incentive for them.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:56.160
Not only have they kind of recouped their
their reputation with all of this, they have

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:59.830
designed the vaccine, but they they're also
making bank.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:03.550
Speaker 1: When you think, oh, let's talk
about that a little bit.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:05.500
Is it that profitable to do this?

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:11.460
And I again, I'm you read so many things that
point in different directions with Pfizer,

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:12.460
for example.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:18.080
From what I've seen, the entire vaccination
program seems to have affected revenue by

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:19.990
about one point eight percent.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:24.070
Now, it's such a big company that one point
eight percent is a lot of dollars.

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:28.830
But in terms of year over year changes, it
doesn't seem dramatic.

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.670
So what how should we be thinking of that
as a profit center?

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:35.600
Speaker 6: We should be thinking long term
because that's what the farmers are doing.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:39.010
They're they're looking at the fact that we've
got strains coming.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:43.690
Not only do we have, you know, a huge percentage
of the world that hasn't received a vaccine

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:44.690
yet.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:45.690
Right.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:51.020
They're thinking when the crisis is over,
they can raise the cost of each dose to the

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:57.040
normal one twenty five, one hundred and fifty
range instead of the 18 to thirty four that

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:00.670
they're getting each dose now for
Speaker 1: the possible future variant booster's,

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:03.650
for example,
Speaker 6: but also for the possible variant

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:07.810
booster's, not only just getting the vaccine
out to the globe, but then following up.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:11.560
And this might be an annual thing for us,
just like the flu.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:12.560
Right.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:17.950
We're not sure how long we're going to remain
have to remain on this vaccination schedule.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:24.830
But also the entire vaccine industry has been
changed by covid-19 in the past.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:28.820
Big Pharma wasn't very interested in it because
it was, you know, low cost.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:31.700
A lot of generics were being used.

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:36.860
And a quick aside here is that India manufactures
80 percent of the drugs that we use in the

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:38.510
United States.

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:42.740
So when we talk about manufacturing elsewhere,
it's not like Big Pharma manufactures its

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:43.900
own drugs anyway.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:46.100
They find that out quite often.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:52.460
And secondly, 70 percent of the materials
for manufacturing drugs come from China.

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:57.610
So the drug supply chain has already been
precarious for several decades.

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:03.260
So the vaccine industry never really appealed
to pharma because it wasn't a big money maker.

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:05.570
You get your smallpox vaccine and that's it.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:11.890
They prefer the very expensive drugs that
have to be taken every day, say cancer drugs

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:15.910
are rare diseases and that's where their big
money is.

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:22.050
And so what Operation Warp Speed did was dump
a whole bunch of money into the vaccine industry

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:27.850
and make it very profitable and busto, a crazy
amount of goodwill on these companies.

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:33.090
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to talk about,
when we think about the trajectory for vaccinating

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:38.390
the world, then we know that there are countries
that we're talking twenty twenty two.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:41.090
We're talking twenty, twenty three different
estimates.

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:42.330
One says twenty, twenty four.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:45.770
Until you really have full vaccination in
some parts of the world.

00:11:45.770 --> 00:11:51.540
Ultimately, will the bottleneck be a manufacturing
and distribution bottleneck?

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:53.810
Will it be an intellectual property bottleneck?

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:57.800
What is the one thing that if it changed,
could move that date up?

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:01.590
Speaker 6: Countries are so different, our
relationships with them are so different.

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:06.860
It's really hard to say, OK, I believe that
it's probably going to be the distribution

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:12.070
and the manufacturing and those special manufacturing
techniques.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.210
And that's just a lack of coordination.

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:20.071
But that's not to say like intellectual property
law is kind of the umbrella term for a whole

00:12:20.071 --> 00:12:21.071
bunch of things.

00:12:21.071 --> 00:12:22.071
Right.

00:12:22.071 --> 00:12:27.280
So if companies are willing to share their
drug patents but not willing to share special

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:30.910
manufacturing techniques, it's the same thing.

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:38.900
They're causing other manufacturing entities
out there from getting up to speed on distributing

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:39.900
this.

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:40.900
Right.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:44.060
Speaker 1: It would be like if I have a really
complicated soufflé, I could give you the

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:45.430
list of ingredients.

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:50.000
But the technique is that what I do with the
ingredients is essentially just as important.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:51.460
Right, exactly.

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:56.890
We've been speaking with Ann Newman, author
and columnist, and I so appreciate your time

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:58.900
today to talk about this really important
topic.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:00.070
Speaker 6: Yeah, I agree.

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:01.070
Thank you for covering this.

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:01.160
Thank you very much.

